module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.positional_citation

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data positional_citation: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "positional_citation" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "positional citation (a `file:line` pointer where a symbol was available — see §3). ",

    "**THE EDIT FORM IS THE SAME CLASS AND IT ALSO WRITES.** An edit anchored on NEIGHBOURING TEXT -- sed on a surrounding line, a string replace keyed on the adjacent entry, an insert before a named sibling -- is a positional handle by another spelling, and it decays for the identical reason: anything that changes above or beside the anchor invalidates it. ",

    "The difference is the consequence. ",

    "A stale citation misleads a reader who can still see the text; a stale ANCHOR silently does NOTHING, because a replace that matches zero times reports success. ",

    "**RECEIPT (sunny-gull-270, 2026-09-02, this carrier).** Appending the row `hedged_benefit_leaves_the_harm_axis_unexamined` needed two edits: the declaration and the roster-list entry. ",

    "The roster append was anchored on `one_refusal_two_destinations,` followed by the closing bracket. ",

    "Between reading the file and writing it, main gained `liveness_probe_read_as_currency` as the new last entry, so the anchor stopped matching, the declaration landed and the roster entry did not, and NOTHING FAILED -- the result would have been a declared-but-unrostered class, exactly the half-applied state a roster cannot detect about itself. ",

    "**WHAT CAUGHT IT IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART, and it is not vigilance: `grep -c` returning 1 where 2 was owed.** The instruction is to CHECK THE RESULT RATHER THAN THE ACTION, because a no-op edit and a successful one are indistinguishable at the actuator -- the same discipline that catches a stale citation by reading `git show origin/main:<path>` rather than the working tree, which a manager on this same exchange used to avoid refuting a correct citation from a pinned checkout. ",

    "Two instances, one root: a read and a write separated by someone else's push. ",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: any edit whose anchor is text the edit does not own. ",

    "State the expected post-condition as a COUNT before applying it, and assert the count afterwards; if you cannot say what the count should become, the edit is not specified.** ",

    "SECOND RECEIPT, AND IT MOVES THIS CLASS'S HARM CEILING (2026-09-04, import-cut lane, found by executing an instrument and refusing two of its rows). The class above is written around stale LINE NUMBERS in citations that a reader consults. This receipt records the same construction fabricating a SUCCESS VERDICT on a program's acceptance criterion, which is a strictly worse harm than a rotted pointer. ",

    "THE SPECIMEN: gunbc.namespace_cut_subject_roster carries HostRustItemProbe { crate_relative_file, item_name } -- a FILE PATH plus a name, which is positional by construction, while its sibling DagDeclarationProbe names module plus declaration and is immune. subject_roster_report reported build_import_adjacency and import_closure_from_adjacency ABSENT from src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs. Both are live at src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/entry_resolve.rs with five call sites, one of them from cli_run.rs itself. They MOVED into a submodule; the probe still named the old file. ",

    "WHY IT IS THE ACCEPTANCE CRITERION AND NOT A READING: ABSENT is the SUCCESS verdict of that instrument -- the namespace cut's exit criterion is the report testifying that the import authorities are gone. So the stale citation did not merely go stale, it manufactured the accepting answer for code that is still running. In the same subject and the same run, compile_entry_on_declared_import_closure_only cited the same file and was correctly PRESENT, which proves the join works and isolates the defect to relocation alone. ",

    "THE GUARD THAT DOES NOT WORK, recorded because it is the obvious one: a file-exists check passes here, since cli_run.rs still exists. Only a search of the whole scanned population distinguishes a MOVED item from a DELETED one, because the two differ nowhere except in where the item now is. Any citation carrying a path -- file path or module path alike -- has this same failure on the next move; replacing one path scheme with a better path scheme is not the repair. ",

    "THE REPAIR IS A REFUSAL ARM, NOT A REPOINT, and this is the transferable half. Repointing the two probes fixes two rows, leaves the construction intact for the next move to re-break, and converts a false ABSENT into a true PRESENT with no record that the instrument was ever misinformed. The landed arm instead searches the population for the item NAME and, when it is found at a path other than the cited one, returns the module's existing honest arm -- SubjectProbeUnobservable -- naming BOTH the cited and the found path, and directing the reader to repair the citation rather than the reading. An instrument that has just discovered its own citation is stale has no sound verdict to give; DESIGN section 5 says it stops the line. ",

    "EXECUTED EVIDENCE, both directions, one run: the two rows turned from ABSENT into the typed stale-citation refusal naming entry_resolve.rs, the discriminating PRESENT stayed PRESENT, and a verdict-level diff of the whole report against the pre-repair run changed EXACTLY those two lines and nothing else. The two false ABSENTs are retained as the class's discriminating RED under DESIGN section 4b(4): they were authorable before the arm existed and they green by construction after it, so they become the permanent regression control rather than retiring. ",

    "TRACKED STALL FOR THIS INSTRUMENT, recorded under DESIGN section 4b(2), which requires a class below its ceiling to NAME ITS TRIGGER so that cannot-climb-further is separated from can-climb-now-but-unbuilt. ",

    "CURRENT RUNG: 2, mechanically preventable. The refusal arm is loud and decidable, which is a real climb from silent and fabricating, but it is VALIDATION. The invalid state -- a probe citing a path the item no longer occupies -- remains perfectly writable, and safety depends on that arm running and staying enrolled. The tell that construction was available is the one DESIGN section 5 gives: the check can be satisfied by editing the ROSTER while the construction still permits the bad state to be written again tomorrow. ",

    "CEILING: 4, structurally impossible -- and the move is NOT a stronger check. It is DELETING crate_relative_file from HostRustItemProbe. A stale citation cannot be written when there is no path field to go stale. The probe would carry ITEM IDENTITY ONLY, and resolution becomes a lookup in the item-grain population gunbc.rust_item_scan already produces over src/v1/stage0/src/, so a MOVE becomes invisible because the file was never part of the question. Membership is therefore decidable from a population that exists today, which is what puts the ceiling at 4 rather than leaving this a ratchet. ",

    "THE ONLY REAL OBJECTION IS NAME COLLISION, AND IT IS THE HONEST REFUSAL: two items of one name resolve to Ambiguous -- a typed refusal rather than a wrong answer -- which is the same shape DagDeclarationProbe already lives with, and is why the sibling arm never had this defect. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: the host probe shape carries item identity with NO path component, resolved against the item-grain population, together with a fixture harness that keeps the refusal authorable. The artifact-shaped near-miss is worth stating because it would be satisfied while the class stood still: adding a Rust MODULE path in place of a file path replaces one positional scheme with a slightly better positional scheme and leaves the class exactly where it is. Any citation carrying a position rots on the next move. ",

    "WHAT THE CLIMB OWES, so it is not discovered as a surprise: at rung 4 the two deliberately-stale rows retained as this class discriminating RED become UNAUTHORABLE in the accepted corpus, because there is no field left to make stale. That does not retire the evidence, it RELOCATES it. DESIGN is explicit that unrepresentable-in-the-accepted-corpus is not the boundary that decides -- a fixture handing the probe a path-shaped citation can still express the refusal, and where no harness can express it, that missing harness is the trigger rather than a permanent ceiling. So the climb owes a fixture-level red, and the do-not-fix block in gunbc.namespace_cut_subject_roster is what tells the next author they owe it. ",

    "RECOGNITION RULE FOR THIS VARIANT: when a check's PASSING verdict is 'the thing is gone', ask what else produces 'not found' -- a moved file, an unread file, a renamed symbol, a scope the scanner never entered. Every one of those is indistinguishable from success unless the check searches the population rather than testing the citation, and each one silently converts a stale pointer into a manufactured acceptance.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
